      "message": "Thank you for the detailed explanations in the previous review.\n\nI have a few remaining questions regarding the new admin and approval mechanism in this patch.\n\n1) Consistency between state changes and notifications\n\nIs it guaranteed that permission-related state changes and their corresponding notifications (such as ConversationProfileUpdated) remain consistent, even in the event of unexpected interruptions?\n\nFor example, what happens if a role change or approval-threshold change is successfully persisted, but the corresponding notification is not emitted because the client crashes or loses power immediately afterwards?\n\nWill the notification or updated state be reliably recovered or propagated after a restart or resynchronization?\n\n2) Concurrent retries and duplicate operations\n\nYou mentioned that an administrator can repeat an action if the device stops between submitting a vote and creating the corresponding resolution.\n\nIs this retry mechanism also safe when multiple attempts happen concurrently or when a previous attempt was actually persisted but its successful completion was not communicated to the client?\n\nCould this result in duplicate resolutions, unintended additional votes, or conflicting state changes?\n\n3) Approval threshold changes and existing pending votes\n\nYou mentioned that a newly approved threshold applies to subsequent resolutions, including resolutions involving votes that were submitted before the threshold change.\n\nIs this behavior intentional, and is it guaranteed to produce consistent and predictable results across all peers, regardless of commit ordering, branch divergence, or synchronization timing?\n\n4) Crash safety, power failures, and recovery\n\nAlthough the previous explanation covers the persistence of votes and recovery from interruptions between vote submission and resolution, I would also like to clarify whether the entire process is safe against unexpected failures at every stage.\n\nFor example, what happens if one or more Jami clients hang, become completely unresponsive, get stuck in a deadlock, freeze during an operation, or stop processing incoming or outgoing Swarm messages while the underlying processes are still running?\n\nCould such situations leave operations pending indefinitely, cause timeouts or incomplete state transitions, or result in inconsistencies when the clients eventually resume processing or are restarted?\n\nWhat happens if one or more clients crash, lose their connections, or the entire system suddenly loses power at any point during vote creation, commit validation, resolution creation, persistence, or notification delivery?\n\nAre all these operations designed to be sufficiently atomic and idempotent to ensure that, after a restart or resynchronization, no partially applied changes, duplicate votes, duplicate resolutions, or inconsistent permission states can occur?\n\nIn particular, is it guaranteed that an operation that was successfully persisted but whose completion was never communicated to the client can be safely retried without causing unintended side effects?\n\nAre these failure scenarios explicitly covered by automated tests?\n\nI appreciate that several of the broader synchronization, persistence, authorization, and recovery scenarios have already been addressed in the previous review and the corresponding forum discussion. I am mainly interested in ensuring that these remaining edge cases are also covered by the implementation and its tests.\n\n5) Interaction with existing implementations and backward compatibility\n\nHave these failure scenarios and consistency requirements also been checked against existing Jami implementations and mechanisms outside the scope of this patch that interact with permission management, Swarm synchronization, commit processing, or related functionality?\n\nCould the new admin and approval mechanism introduce incompatibilities or expose existing weaknesses in these components, particularly regarding crash recovery, interrupted operations, client hangs, concurrent processing, or state synchronization?\n\nWhere necessary, have corresponding safeguards or adjustments to existing implementations also been considered or implemented to ensure that the new mechanism works reliably across the entire system, rather than only within the scope of this patch?\n\nIn other words, are the new mechanisms fully compatible with the existing permission management and recovery architecture, and have potential issues in related components outside this patch\u0027s scope also been identified and addressed where necessary?",
